# Untitled

Coral-like cobalt selenide/carbon nanosheet arrays attached on carbon nanofibers for high-rate sodium-ion storage
Abstract Cobalt selenide \((\mathbf{CoSe}_{2})\) has become a promising anode material for sodium-ion batteries (SIBs) due to its stable chemical properties, environmental friendliness, and high theoretical capacity. However, the undesirable rate capacity and cycle stability of the anode materials largely limit its applications for SIBs due to the relatively low electronic conductivity and huge volume change during the \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) insertion/extraction. In this study, electrostatic spinning combined with a wet chemical method is employed to synthesize coral-like composite material \((\mathrm{CNF}@c\mathrm{-CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C})\), which is composed of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\)/carbon nanosheet arrays \((\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C})\) and carbon nanofibers (CNFs). \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) nanoflakes derived from metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with high surface area and the porous structure can inhibit the pulverization and amorphization of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) during charge and discharge processes, thus significantly keeping the stability of the microstructure. CNF can limit the overgrowth of nanosheets and serve as a conductive skeleton. Compared to two-dimensional \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) nanoflakes and pure \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) nanoparticles, the composite can expose more active sites and effectively accelerate the
diffusion of \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\), which displays enhanced rate capability \((266.5\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) at \(5.0\ \mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) and cycling stability \((268.3\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) after 100 cycles at \(1.0\ \mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}}\)). Moreover, the rational preparation strategy for metal selenide-based heterostructure material presents a new way for high-performance SIBs.
Keywords Carbon nanofiber (CNF); Cobalt selenide \((\mathbf{CoSe}_{2})\); Electrostatic spinning; Sodium-ion storage
## 1 Introduction
The vigorous development of clean energy is highly required to deal with the challenges of environmental pollution and energy crisis in recent years [1–5]. Because of the instability and discontinuity of clean energy, efficient and stable energy storage devices have become the focus of people's research [6, 7]. Among many energy storage devices, lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have been widely used in portable electronic products, electric vehicles, and other fields [8–11]. Unfortunately, the future application of lithium resources is limited by its scarcity, uneven distribution, and high cost [12–14]. It is urgent to find alternatives. Sodium-ion batteries (SIBs) are considered ideal substitutes due to the rich sodium resources, low cost, and suitable redox potential [15–17]. Nevertheless, many widely used materials in commercial LIBs (i.e., graphite) can hardly be directly applied to SIBs because of the mismatched lattice spacing between metal ions and electrode materials [18]. The larger radius of \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) \((0.102\ \mathrm{nm})\) than \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) \((0.076\ \mathrm{nm})\) seriously hinders the sodiation/desodiation rates in SIBs electrode materials, which results in fast capacity attenuation, inferior rate
performance, and poor cycling stability [19]. Thus, it is urgent to seek suitable electrode materials for SIBs.
Recently, transitional metal chalcogenides (TMCs), especially cobalt selenide \(\mathrm{(CoSe}_{2})\), have attracted people's attention as a promising anode material for sodium-ion storage due to the moderate reaction platform, high theoretical capacity, and easy preparation [20–22]. However, the limited conductivity, heavy self-aggregation, and volume expansion of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) nanoparticles during the long time charging and discharging process could result in poor rate performance and severe capacity fading, which restricts its further application [23, 24]. Thus, it would be highly valuable to explore \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\)-based electrode materials with efficient electron transportation, high structural tolerance, and superior sufficient active sites for high-performance sodium-ion storage.
The derivatives of metal-organic framework (MOF) with ultrahigh porosity and high specific area are greatly focused because they can facilitate the infiltration of electrolyte ions into the electrode material, which may simultaneously improve the mechanical stability and electrochemistry stability [25–29]. However, most of the MOFs-derived materials prepared by direct carbonization methods usually present a reduced effective surface area and depressed graphitic degree of the synthesized samples, which displays low specific capacity and poor rate capability [30].
Carbon nanofibers (CNFs) with unique one-dimensional structure, favorable conductivity, excellent mechanical property, and chemical stability can be used as a conductive carbon skeleton to overcome the poor electron conductive of MOF-derived electrode materials and prevent MOFs from collapsing during the carbonization process [29]. Profiting from the advantages of CNF and MOFs derivants, the MOFs-based CNF electrode materials have attracted extensive attention for metal-ion batteries [31–33]. Few related electrode materials have also been investigated for SIBs [31, 32]. The hierarchical porous structure of MOFs derivants combined with CNF can easily promote the electrolyte diffusion and charge transfer, thereby improving the electrochemical reaction of the active materials. The skeleton structure can create an excellent mechanical robustness under a repeated sodiation/desodiation process. The introduction of carbon materials can improve electrode stabilization and ion insertion/extraction kinetics [34]. The morphological construction of the composites can avoid the agglomeration of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\), expose more active sites, and effectively accelerate the diffusion of \(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\) [35]. Therefore, the construction of a nanocomposite composed of MOF-derived \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) on CNFs skeleton may exhibit extraordinary electrochemical properties, which is highly desired. Unfortunately, it remains a great challenge to the growth of abundant MOF-derived
\(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) with regular and orderly nanostructure on the surface of CNFs possibly due to the poor atomic affinity and high interfacial contact resistance between CNFs skeleton and MOF complexes.
In this consideration, the heterogeneous coral-like \(\mathrm{CNF}@\mathrm{c}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) composite has been rationally constructed via a simple strategy using leaf-like zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (Co-ZIF-L) nanosheets and the methylimidazole/polyacrylonitrile (MIM/PAN) nanofibers as dual templates. The as-prepared \(\mathrm{CNF}@\mathrm{c}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) composite is made of CNF and two-dimensional (2D) nanosheets with different edge lengths and thicknesses. The unique heterostructure composite material delivers a high capacity of \(268.3\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) after 100 cycles at \(1.0\ \mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and an excellent rate capacity of \(266.5\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) at \(5.0\ \mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}}\). The excellent performance can be mainly attributed to the following three points. First, the ZIF-L-derived carbon can inhibit the self-aggregation of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) nanoparticles during the cycling process. Second, CNF can enhance the electronic conductivity of the composite material. Third, the coral-like composite can expose more active sites, increasing the contact area between the electrolyte and the electrode material. Correspondingly, \(\mathrm{CNF}@\mathrm{c}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) composite material may also be used as an electrode for LIBs, supercapacitors, Li-S batteries, etc.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate \((\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), \(98.5\ \mathrm{wt}\%)\), 2-methylimidazole (2-MIM, Hmim, \(\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{N}_{2}\), \(98\ \mathrm{wt}\%)\), N-N dimethylformamide (DMF, \(99.5\ \mathrm{wt}\%)\), polyacrylonitrile (PAN, average molecular weight \((M_{\mathrm{w}})=150{,}000)\), selenium \((99.9\ \mathrm{wt}\%)\), \(\mathrm{Na}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}\) and hydrazine hydrate \((\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\) were purchased from Shanghai Aladdin BioChem Technology Co., Ltd. All chemicals were of analytical grade and used without further purification.
## 2.2 Synthesis of MIM/PAN nanofibers membrane
MIM/PAN nanofibers membrane was prepared by an electrospinning technology. Typically, a precursor solution was prepared by dissolving \(0.35\ \mathrm{g}\) PAN and \(0.15\ \mathrm{g}\) 2-MIM into \(5\ \mathrm{ml}\) DMF. Then, a light yellow spinning solution was obtained by heating the precursor solution in an oil bath at \(80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\ \mathrm{h}\) and further stirred continuously overnight at room temperature. After that, the light yellow spinning solution was loaded into a \(5\ \mathrm{ml}\) plastic syringe equipped with a 21-gauge stainless steel nozzle and ejected onto a drum collector covered with aluminum foil by electrospun at a flow rate of \(2\ \mathrm{ml\cdot h^{-1}}\). During the electrospinning
process, the distance between the tip of the nozzle and the Al collector was maintained at \(20~\mathrm{cm}\). The applied voltage between the collector and the syringe tip was \(14~\mathrm{kV}\). Finally, the white MIM/PAN nanofibers membrane was prepared after the electrospinning process for \(3\mathrm{~h~}\) and stabilized at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12~\mathrm{h}\) in a vacuum oven.
## 2.3 Synthesis of leaf-like MIM/PAN@Co-ZIF-L membrane
\(1.46\mathrm{~g~}\,\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(3.28\mathrm{~g~}\) 2-MIM were firstly dissolved in \(100~\mathrm{ml}\) deionized water, respectively. Then, the 2-MIM solution was quickly added to a \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{N}-\) \(\mathrm{O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) solution and stirred at room temperature to form a mixture solution. Finally, a \(5\ \mathrm{cm}\,\times\,5\ \mathrm{cm}\) MIM/ PAN nanofibers membrane was directly immersed into the mixture solution and aged at room temperature for \(60\;\mathrm{min}\). After the washing and drying, a purple MIM/PAN@CoZIF-L membrane was successfully obtained.
## 2.4 Synthesis of CNF@c-CoSe2/C and CoSe2/C composite
MIM/PAN@Co-ZIF-L membrane was pre-oxidized at \(280~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(90\ \mathrm{min}\) in air and then annealed at \(600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) under the protection of nitrogen atmosphere. The annealed product was marked as \(\mathrm{CNF}@C o/C\). \(100~\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{CNF}@C o/C\) compound and \(200~\mathrm{mg}\) of selenium powders were placed into two separate rectangular crucibles and placed in the middle of a slender quartz tube. The crucible containing selenium powders was placed in front, and the distance between the two crucibles was set to be \(5~\mathrm{cm}\). After that, the tube furnace was heated to \(400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and kept at this temperature for \(2~\mathrm{{h}}\) under an argon atmosphere. After cooling down to room temperature, \(\mathrm{CNF}@c\mathrm{-CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) composite was successfully prepared. For comparison, the \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) composite was prepared by the same process, except that MIM/PAN@Co-ZIF-L membrane was replaced by Co-ZIF-L nanosheets.
## 2.5 Synthesis of \(\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}\) nanoparticles
\(1\ \mathrm{mmol}\) selenium powders were dissolved in \(5~\mathrm{ml}\) hydrazine hydrate \(\mathrm{(N_{2}H_{4}{\cdot}H_{2}O)}\) solution to form Solution A. \(2\ \mathrm{mmol}\) \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) solution was dissolved in \(30~\mathrm{ml}\) deionized water to form Solution B. Then, Solutions A and B were mixed under magnetic agitation for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\). Next, the above solution was transferred to a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and heated in an oven at \(160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) (marked as \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) nanoparticles). The as-prepared \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) nanoparticles sample was obtained by centrifugation, washing, and drying process.
## 2.6 Preparation of full-cell
The sodium-ion full-cell was assembled using the \(\mathrm{CNF}@c-\) \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) electrode as an anode and a \(\mathrm{Na}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}\) electrode as a cathode. The anode electrode was presodiated in a half-cell for three cycles at \(0.1\mathrm{~A{\cdot}g^{-1}}\). Then, the weight ratio between \(\mathrm{Na}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{CNF}@c\mathrm{-CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) was 2:1.
## 2.7 Electrochemical measurements
The electrochemical performance of the samples as anodes for SIBs was evaluated by using 2032-type coin cells. The coin cells were assembled in an Ar-filled glove box (both \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) content \(<0.1\,\times\,10^{-4}\)). For Na-ion half-cells, the anode was prepared by mixing \(80~\mathrm{wt}\%\) active materials, \(10~\mathrm{wt}\%\) carbon black, and \(10~\mathrm{wt}\%\) polyvinylidene fluoride in 1-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP). The slurry was cast onto copper foil and dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12~\mathrm{h}\); then, the foil was punched into small disks. The mass loading of active materials varied from 0.8 to \(1.2~\mathrm{mg\cdot cm}^{-2}\). Na metal and glass microfiber were used as the counter electrode and separator, respectively. The electrolyte was produced by dissolving \(1.0~\mathrm{mol\cdot L^{-1}}~\mathrm{NaClO_{4}}\) in a mixture of ethylene carbonate/dimethyl carbonate (EC/DMC; 1:1 by volume) and \(5\%\) fluoroethylene carbonates (FEC) as the additive. The discharge/charge characteristics of the samples were investigated by cycling the cell over a potential range of \(0.01{-}3.0~\mathrm{V}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Na^{+}/N a}\)) at various current densities. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves and electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) were measured by Gamry Interface 5000P electrochemical workstation. The frequency range of EIS was \(0.01~\mathrm{Hz}{-}100~\mathrm{kHz}\) with \(5.0~\mathrm{mV}\) for amplitude. Galvanostatic charge/discharge (GCD) measurements and galvanostatic intermittent titration technique (GITT) were performed by using the LAND CT2001A battery test system. GITT tests were recorded at \(0.1~\mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) with a duration of the current pulse of \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) and a rest interval time of \(60~\mathrm{min}\). All the tests were carried out at room temperature.
## 2.8 Characterization
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM, JSM-7800) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, Tecnai F20, FEI) were performed to analyze the morphology and microstructure of the samples. X-ray diffraction (XRD, German Bruker D8 with \(\mathrm{Cu}~\mathrm{K}\alpha\)) was used to examine the crystal structure of the samples. X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) were recorded by using an ESCALAB 250Xi spectrometer equipped with a monochromatic Al \(\mathrm{K}\alpha\) source. \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption–desorption isotherms were tested by using an intelligent gravimetric analyzer Autosorb-iQ (Quantachrome). The content of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) in the composite
material was measured with a thermogravimetric (TG) analyzer within \(30{-}800\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a heating rate of \(10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) in air.
## 3 Results and discussion
The preparation process of \(\mathrm{CNF}@\mathrm{c}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) sample is shown in Scheme 1. A MIM/PAN nanofibers membrane was obtained by an electrospinning technology using a mixture of 2-MIM, PAN and DMF as the electrospinning solution. Owing to the strong binding force between 2-MIM and \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\), Co-ZIF-L nanoparticles can quickly nucleate and easily grow on the surface of MIM/PAN nanofibers, forming a MIM/PAN@Co-ZIF-L nanosheets/-nanofibers composite membrane. Subsequently, the \(\mathrm{CNF}@\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{C}\) membrane was gained after the pre-oxidative stabilization and carbonization process. In this process, CNF was obtained by the carbonization of MIM/PAN nanofibers, which not only maintained the integrity of the conductive skeleton but also gained excellent electronic conductivity. In the meantime, Co nanoparticles were achieved due to the reduction of Co-ZIF-L [32]. Finally, the hierarchical \(\mathrm{CNF}@\mathrm{c}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) sample was obtained after a selenylation process.
The morphological and microstructural characterizations of MIM/PAN@Co-ZIF-L, \(\mathrm{CNF}@\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{CNF}@\mathrm{c}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) samples were examined by SEM. The average diameter of MIM/PAN nanofibers was measured to be \(150\ \mathrm{nm}\), as shown in Fig. S1a. The smooth surface and uniform diameter of MIM/PAN nanofibers indicate an efficient route for electron transportation after the annealing treatments, which may improve the conductivity of the composite material [32]. 2D Co-ZIF-L nanosheets with a smooth surface are densely grown on each of MIM/PAN nanofibers (Fig. 1a), indicating the successful preparation of the hierarchical MIM/PAN@Co-ZIF-L sample. The average length and thickness of Co-ZIF-L nanosheets are measured to be \(1\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) and \(100\ \mathrm{nm}\), respectively. The size of the obtained Co-ZIF-L nanosheets (about \(1\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\)) in MIM/PAN@Co-ZIF-L sample is smaller than that of Co-ZIF-L powder (about \(40\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\), Fig. S1b), suggesting that the growth of MOF can be largely restricted when using the polymer binder as a substrate. After annealing treatment, the nanosheets/nanofibers hierarchical morphology is well preserved in \(\mathrm{CNF}@\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{C}\) sample (Fig. 1b), confirming the high structural stability of the sample. The surface roughness of \(\mathrm{CNF}@\mathrm{c}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) composite is greatly changed after selenylation (Fig. 1c). In addition, the microstructure of each nanosheet can be observed, which is similar to that of a coral (inset of Fig. 1c). The coral-like microstructure is
composed of a series of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) nanoparticles (about \(60~\mathrm{nm}\)) and a thick carbon skeleton (Fig. 1d). The stable heterostructure of \(\mathrm{CNF@c-CoSe_{2}/C}\) material may facilitate the maximum exposure of active sites, provide sufficient electrons transmission routes, and enhance the contact area between the electrolyte and the active material, ultimately improving its sodium storage performance [36]. The severe aggregation of \(\mathrm{Co/C}\), \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) samples is further observed by SEM in Fig. S1c–e, which further emphasizes the significance of carbon skeleton.
The heterostructure of \(\mathrm{CNF@c-CoSe_{2}/C}\) composite is further analyzed by TEM image in Fig. 2a. The blue dashed area represents the morphology of a \(\mathrm{c-CoSe_{2}/C}\) nanosheet and the green dashed area represents a CNF nanorod, indicating the successful construction of three-dimension (3D) structure. The 2D thin layers of \(\mathrm{c-CoSe_{2}/C}\) composite with numerous nanoparticles and nanoporous (orange dotted lines areas) on the surface/interface of each nanosheet can be seen in TEM image in Fig. 2b, which is consistent with the observed morphology in SEM and demonstrates the uniform distribution of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) nanoparticles in ZIF-derived carbon material. Selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern of \(\mathrm{c-CoSe_{2}/C}\) nanosheet (inset of Fig. 2b) displays weak diffraction rings, which should belong to (220) and (111) planes of orthogonal \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\). In Fig. 2c, high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image further manifests the distinct layers of amorphous carbon
and crystallized \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\), indicating the successful formation of \(\mathrm{c-CoSe_{2}/C}\) composite. The clear fringes with a lattice spacing of about \(0.29~\mathrm{nm}\) can be measured, corresponding to (031) plane of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) (Fig. 2d). C, N, Co and Se elements can be observed in Fig. 2e throughout the nanosheet, further confirming the uniform distribution of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) in the ZIF-derived carbon. The atomic ratio of \(\mathrm{Co:Se}\) measured from energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) spectrum in Fig. 2e is 19:41 (inset table of Fig. 2f), which is close to standard metrological proportions of the crystallized \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\). The content of \(\mathbf{N}\) element is approaching 0, indicating that a small amount of \(\mathbf{N}\) is doped into ZIF-L nanosheets.
To further explore the content of carbon in the complex, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) results were measured in air atmosphere (Fig. S2). The weight loss below \(215~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) should be attributed to the evaporation of water absorbed on the surface of the sample. About \(65.5\%\) of weight loss was detected from 215 to \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and the calculated carbon is \(12.4\%\) based on Eq. (1) [37]. The slight weight increase between 215 to \(280~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) can be ascribed to the conversion of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) into \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\). The major weight loss that occurred between 280 and \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) can be regarded as the oxidation of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) into \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\), the combustion of carbon material, and the conversion \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\) into \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) [38]. The specific surface area and pore size distribution of \(\mathrm{CNF@c-CoSe_{2}/C}\) composite measured by nitrogen adsorption–desorption
isotherms are provided in Fig. S3. The isotherms of the composite could be classified as IV type. Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) specific surface area of the composite is \(21.7\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , and the average pore size is \(3.44~\mathrm{nm}\) . The composite with a mesoporous structure is beneficial for promoting the infiltration of the electrolyte and ion transport.
