The as-prepared \(\mathrm{CNF@c-CoSe_{2}/C}\) composite is analyzed by XRD (Fig. 3a). Some sharp peaks ranging from \(10^{\circ}\) to \(40^{\circ}\) (marked with \(\star\star\) ) are assigned to the characteristic peaks of Co-ZIF-L, proving the successful synthesis of MIM/PAN \(@\) Co-ZIF-L [39]. The diffraction peaks
(marked with \(\bullet\bullet\bigcirc)\) at \(44.2^{\circ}\) , \(51.5^{\circ}\) and \(75.85^{\circ}\) of \(\mathrm{CNF@Co/C}\) sample are ascribed to (111), (200) and (220) lattice facets of cubic crystal system Co (JCPDS No. 89-4037). The entire diffraction peaks at \(30.6^{\circ}\) , \(34.2^{\circ}\) , \(35.7^{\circ}\) , \(36.8^{\circ}\) , \(40.2^{\circ}\) , \(43.8^{\circ}\) , \(47.5^{\circ}\) , \(50.1^{\circ}\) , \(53.2^{\circ}\) , \(55.2^{\circ}\) , \(56.8^{\circ}\) , \(59.1^{\circ}\) and \(63.1^{\circ}\) match well with (101), (111), (120), (200), (210), (121), (211), (002), (031), (221), (131), (310) and (122) planes of the orthorhombic \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) (JCPDS No. 53-0449), demonstrating the successful preparation of \(\mathrm{CNF@c-CoSe_{2}/C}\) composite. Relatively, similar diffraction peaks of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) samples can also be detected, as shown in Fig. S4a.
XPS further proves the chemical composition and valence bond of \(\mathrm{CNF@c-CoSe_{2}/C}\) composite. The survey XPS spectrum reveals the existence of Co 2p, Se 3d and C 1s in \(\mathrm{CNF@c-CoSe_{2}/C}\) composite (Fig. S4b). In Co 2p spectrum (Fig. 3b), the coexistence of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) in \(\mathrm{CNF@c-CoSe_{2}/C}\) composite is evidenced by four fitted peaks at \(780.0~\mathrm{eV}\) \((\mathrm{Co}^{2+}~2\mathrm{p}_{3/2})\) , \(795.0~\mathrm{eV}\) \((\mathrm{Co}^{2+}~2\mathrm{p}_{1/2})\) ,
\(778.0~\mathrm{eV}\) \((\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}^{3+}\) , \(2{\bf p}_{3/2})\) and \(793.0~\mathrm{eV}\) \((\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}^{3+}\) , \(2{\bf p}_{1/2})\) . And the peak at 784.8 and \(799.8~\mathrm{eV}\) belongs to the satellite peaks of \(\mathrm{{Co}}^{2+}\) [40]. The \(\mathrm{{Co}}^{3+}\) peaks are attributed to the partial surface oxidation of the sample in air [41]. Two peaks at binding energies of \(54.2~\mathrm{eV}\) (Se \(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2})\) and \(55.2~\mathrm{eV}\) (Se \(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2})\) can be attributed in Se 3d XPS spectrum (Fig. 3c), which is the metal-Se interactions of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) [42]. The additional peak at \(58.77~\mathrm{eV}\) is \(\mathrm{Se}{-}\mathrm{O}\) bond formed by the surface oxidation of nonmetallic Se [41, 42]. C 1s spectrum in Fig. 3d can be separated into three peaks corresponding to C–C, C–N and C–O bonds at 283.8, 284.5 and \(286.2~\mathrm{eV}\) , respectively [43].
\(\mathrm{CNF}@c\mathrm{-CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) composite as an anode material is explored via assembling coin-type half-cells for SIBs. The initial three cycles of CV curves for the \(\mathrm{CNF}@c\mathrm{-CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) anode are measured in the potential range of \(0.01{-}3.0\ \mathrm{V}\) at the scan rate of \(0.1\ \mathrm{mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\) (Fig. 4a). For the \(\mathrm{CNF}@c\mathrm{-}\) \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) anode, the first cathodic peak at around \(0.99~\mathrm{V}\) could be ascribed to the activation process of the anode and the formation of a solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) film [41, 44]. In the first anodic scan, there is a distinct anodic peak at \(1.93~\mathrm{V}\) , corresponding to the desodiation process.
Clearly, the following cathodic scans show three reduction peaks at around 1.43, 1.12 and \(0.69~\mathrm{V}\) , which probably correspond to the \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) intercalation \((\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}+x\mathrm{Na}^{+}+\) \(x\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{Na}_{x}\mathrm{CoSe}_{2})\) and the conversion reactions of CoSe and Co \((\mathrm{Na}_{x}\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}+x\mathrm{Na}^{+}+x\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{CoSe}+\mathrm{Na}_{2x}\mathrm{Se},\) \(\mathrm{CoSe}+2\mathrm{Na}^{+}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{Co}+\mathrm{NaSe})\) [35, 44]. One outstanding oxidation peak appears at about \(1.93~\mathrm{V}\) , which is attributed to the desodiation process and the formation of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) [6]. As depicted in Fig. 4b, the discharge–charge curves of the \(\mathrm{CNF}@c\mathrm{-CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) anode for the first three cycles are recorded at \(0.1~\mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) . In the first discharge cycle, an obvious voltage plateau at \(0.99~\mathrm{V}\) is observed, which is in good agreement with the result from CV curves in Fig. 4a. For comparison, CV and discharge–charge curves of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) anodes are shown in Fig. S5. The results signify that the sodium-ion storage behavior is the same as that of the \(\mathrm{CNF}@c\mathrm{-CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) anode. The rate capability of the \(\mathrm{CNF}@c\mathrm{-CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) , \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) anodes is shown in Fig. 4c. It can be seen that the discharge capacities of the \(\mathrm{CNF}@c\mathrm{-CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) anode are 386.3, 374.8, 342.6, 325.8, 305.8 and \(266.5~\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) at 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0 and \(5.0~\mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) , respectively. Moreover, the \(\mathrm{CNF}@c\mathrm{-}\)
\(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) anode displays the best rate performance among the other two anodes, indicating that CNF as a conductive skeleton not only promotes ion transport but also exposes more active sites for the electrochemical reaction [45]. Additionally, the \(\mathrm{CNF@c\mathrm{-}C o S e_{2}/C}\) anode exhibits a higher and most stable specific capacity of \(268.3\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) as compared to the \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) \((154.6\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) and \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) \((29.6\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\), and CNF \((152.8\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) anodes at \(1.0\ \mathsf{A\cdot g}^{-1}\) after 100 cycles (Fig. 4d), suggesting that MOF
derived carbon can ease the volume charge during the sodiation and desodiation process [25]. To verify the structural integrity of the \(\mathrm{CNF@c\mathrm{-}C o S e_{2}/C}\) electrode after cycling, SEM and TEM images of the electrode after 100 cycles are provided in Fig. S6. \(\mathrm{CNF@c\mathrm{-}C o S e_{2}/C}\) sample retains its original heterostructure with slight surface changes after cycles (Fig. S6a–c) due to the volume expansion of \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) during the sodiation/desodiation process. The cycled \(\mathrm{CNF@c\mathrm{-}C o S e_{2}/C}\) anode is further
analyzed by TEM images, as shown in Fig. S6d–f. The blue dashed area in Fig. S6d represents c-CoSe2/C nanosheets and the green dashed area is CNF nanorod. Numerous nanocrystals and nanoporous can be observed (orange dotted lines areas) on the surface/interface of the nanosheet with uniform distributions (Fig. S6e). Moreover, a lattice spacing of about \( 0.19\ \mathrm{nm} \) can also be measured in Fig. S6f, corresponding to (111) plane of \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2} \). The heterostructure material with numerous nanocrystals and nanoporous after cycles indicates the outstanding structural stability and excellent \( \mathrm{Na^{+}} \)storage properties of the anode material. The outstanding electrochemical performance of the CNF@c-CoSe2/C anode in terms of the improved rate capability and cycling stability can be highlighted as the above advantages.
It is well known that the energy storage performance of a sodium-ion battery mainly depends on the \( \mathrm{Na^{+}} \)diffusion ability of the electrode materials. Therefore, EIS was conducted to further explore the diffusion kinetics of our self-prepared electrodes. EIS results for the CNF@c
\( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \), \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) and pure \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2} \) anodes before circulation are shown in Fig. 5a. All the curves exhibit a semicircle in high frequency and a straight line in low frequency. According to the semicircle size in the Nyquist plots, the CNF@c-CoSe2/C anode displays the minimum charge transfer resistance \( (R_{\mathrm{ct}}) \) (Fig. 5a), suggesting that the combination of CNF and MOF-derived carbon materials can efficiently facilitate fast electron transfer by enhancing the conductivity of the electrode material and increase the contact area between the active material and electrolyte [43, 46]. By fitting the low-frequency region of the Nyquist plot, the Warburg factor \( (\sigma) \) is calculated to be 8.60, 13.74, and \( 25.56\ \Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1/2} \) for the CNF@c-CoSe2/C, \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) and pure \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2} \) anodes, respectively. Notably, the CNF@c-CoSe2/C anode possesses a smaller Warburg impedance coefficient compared to the other two electrodes, indicating a faster \( \mathrm{Na^{+}} \)diffusion ability (Fig. 5b).
To further explain the diffusion ability of the CNF@c\( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) and \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) anodes, GITT in Fig. 5c is used to confirm \( \mathrm{Na^{+}} \)diffusion coefficient \( (D_{\mathrm{Na^{+}}}) \) of the active
electrode at different voltages, which is calculated by the following equation [47]:
\[
D=\frac{4L^2}{\pi\tau}\left(\frac{\Delta E_{\mathrm{s}}}{\Delta E_{\tau}}\right)^2
\]
where \(\tau\) is the constant current pulse time, \(L\) denotes \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) diffusion length for a compact electrode, which is equal to the average thickness of the electrode; \(\Delta E_{\mathrm{s}}\) is the difference value between two continuous stable voltages after relaxation, \(\Delta E_{\tau}\) is the instantaneous voltage changes in a single titration step. The \(\mathrm{CNF@c-CoSe_{2}/C}\) anode displays a higher \(D_{\mathrm{Na^{+}}}\) value than that of the \(\mathrm{CoSe_{2}/C}\) anode in both the discharge and charge processes, further suggesting that 3D conductive network architecture material has superior sodium-ion storage dynamics (Fig. S7a, b).
The \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) storage mechanism of the \(\mathrm{CNF@c-CoSe_{2}/C}\) anode is further investigated by CV curves with different scan rates from 0.2 to \(1.0\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) , as shown in Fig. 6a. Three reduction peaks at 1.43, 1.12 and \(0.69~\mathrm{V}\) and one oxidation peak at \(1.93\mathrm{~V~}\) are observed at a scan rate of \(0.1\ \mathrm{mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\) and reappeared as scan rates increased. In
terms of CV measurements, the relationship between peak current \((i)\) and scan rate \((\nu)\) can be described as follows [48]:
\[
\begin{array}{l}{i=a\nu^b}\\{\mathrm{lgi}=b\mathrm{lgv}+\mathrm{lga}}\end{array}
\]
where \(a\) and \(b\) are both variable parameters. The diffusion or pseudocapacitance control behaviors can be judged according to the value of \(b\) [49]. When \(b\) value is close to 1, the electrochemical system is mainly dominated by pseudocapacitance. When \(b\) value is 0.5, the diffusioncontrolled behavior dominates the charge–discharge process. The current densities of three reduction Peaks \(1-\) 3 and one oxidation Peak 4 are collected from CV curves to calculate the value of b, as shown in Fig. 6b. All the \(b\) values are higher than 0.5, suggesting that the \(\mathrm{CNF@CoSe_{2}/C}\) anode is mainly dominated by pseudocapacitance electrochemical reactions. In addition, the pseudocapacitive and diffusion-controlled contributions can be quantitatively calculated based on Eq. (5) [41, 50]:
\[
i=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}\nu^{1/2}
\]
Such outstanding electrochemical properties of the \( \mathrm{CNF}@c \) - \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) anode for SIB can be attributed to the following reasons: first, \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2} \) nanoparticles (about \( 60\ \mathrm{nm} \)) uniformly embedded into ZIF-derived carbon (Fig. 1d) can ease the volume change of \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2} \) during the cycling process, which improved the stability of the electrode (Fig. 4d). Second, both the ZIF-derived carbon and CNF can increase the conductivity of the electrode (Figs. 2c and 3a) and the uniform CNF substrate can prohibit the overgrowth of ZIF-L (Fig. 1c), which enhances the utilization rate of active materials and results in excellent rate performance and high specific capacity (Fig. 4c). Third, the 3D conductive network architecture of the complex electrode displays low values of \( R_{\mathrm{ct}} \) and \( \sigma \) (Fig. 5a, b) and a high \( D_{\mathrm{Na^{+}}} \) values (Fig. S7a, b), indicating a fast electron transfer rate and high \( \mathrm{Na^{+}} \) diffusion ability.
The \( \mathrm{Na^{+}} \) storage property of the \( \mathrm{CNF}@c \) - \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C} // \mathrm{Na}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}@C \) full-cell is investigated, as shown in Fig. S8a. The discharge–charge curves of the full-cell for the first three cycles are recorded at \( 0.1\ \mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) between 0.5 and \( 3.6\ \mathrm{V} \). The full-cell exhibits charge and discharge capacities of \( 339.7\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) and \( 325.3\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) in the first cycle (based on the anode mass). A stable charge and discharge process of the full-cell can be seen from the first three cycles. The rate property of the full-cell is shown in Fig. S8b. It can be seen that the average discharge capacities of the full-cell are 311.3, 236.9, 191.0 and \( 160.2\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) at 0.1, 0.2, 0.5 and \( 1.0\ \mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}} \), respectively. And the discharge capacity of the full-cell is \( 223.3\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) after 30 cycles at \( 0.5\ \mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}} \), corresponding to an average coulombic efficiency of \( 98.2\% \) (Fig. S8c). The high Coulombic efficiency of the full-cell provides a reliable prospect for further improvement and enhancement of sodium-ion storage.
## 4 Conclusion
In summary, the heterostructure \( \mathrm{CNF}@c \) - \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) composite as an anode material for SIBs was successfully prepared via an electrospinning method and a series of annealing treatments using Co-ZIF-L and MIM/PAN as dual templates. Because of the high theoretical capacity of \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2} \), the good conductivity of CNF and ZIF-derived carbon, as well as the multiple active sites of 3D coral-like structure, the unique heterostructure electrode delivers a high reversible discharge storage capacity of \( 266.5\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) at \( 5.0\ \mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) and an outstanding rate capacity. This new dual-template strategy can also be used to prepare other metal selenide-based heterostructure materials for metal-Se batteries, supercapacitors, Zn-ion batteries, etc.
